          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. What does the diagnosis mean, what does guidance describe as first-line therapy, and which code covers it?",
          "answer": "**What the diagnosis means.** Type 2 diabetes is a condition where the body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, and the pancreas cannot keep up with the extra insulin needed. Blood glucose stays above the normal range. It usually develops gradually over years, and many people have no symptoms at diagnosis. (Source: *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*, general clinical reference.)\n\n**First-line therapy.** The *Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management* guidance (National diabetes association standards of care, educational summary) says the first-line choice now depends on other conditions you may have, not only on your blood test results. Where there is no other condition that changes the choice, it describes a structured lifestyle programme alongside a medicine, usually one long-used and low-cost. It names that medicine, but I cannot tell you to take it or what dose to use. Your clinician chooses the treatment with you. The same guidance says kidney function is checked before starting and periodically after. The passage I received cuts off there, so I cannot tell you the full list of options or the other conditions that change the choice.\n\n**The code.** Type 2 diabetes is **E11** in ICD-10-CM (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*, educational summary). Taking insulin does not change the code; it is still E11, with insulin use recorded separately. Clinical staff assign codes for records and billing, so you do not need to pick one yourself.\n\n**What these sources do not cover:** blood glucose targets, monitoring schedules, diet details, and what to expect day to day. Your diagnosing clinician is the right person to ask about these and about which treatment suits you.\n\n*Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.*",
